WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.386 --> 00:00:05.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.050
NARRATOR: Wilbur
and Orville Wright.

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.210
The first men in the
history of the world

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.730
to pilot a heavier
than air craft

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:15.375
and sustain flight
under power and control.

00:00:15.375 --> 00:00:16.750
MINDELL: The Wright
brothers were

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.890
great pioneers in the
way they approached

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.760
the problem of flight.

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.270
NARRATOR: The speed
about 40 miles an hour,

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:27.300
altitude, as you see,
about 75 to a 100 feet.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.550
MINDELL: There are a lot of
different ideas about what

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.810
an airplane should look like
and what the wings should

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:35.400
look like, but there's not
all that much data out there.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.100
They combined empirical
studies in a wind tunnel

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.370
with the actual desire
to build an airplane

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.140
and learn how to fly it.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.860
MIT brings that combination
to the next level,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.570
to really build the tools, build
the instruments, take the data,

00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.436
compare it to the
physical principles,

00:00:53.436 --> 00:00:54.810
and understand
what you're doing.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.440
ROY: What we have done is
largely focused on the software

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:03.440
challenges.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.190
What are the algorithms that
allow different kinds of sensor

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:08.551
information to be brought
together and fused

00:01:08.551 --> 00:01:10.550
into consistent estimate
of where the vehicle is

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.660
and what's around it?

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.930
How do you plan trajectories
through the environment that

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:17.645
are safe, that allow the
vehicle to move quickly,

00:01:17.645 --> 00:01:19.310
and without collision?

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:20.990
How do you incorporate
information

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.850
about what's in the world?

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:24.600
How do you incorporate
sensor information

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.280
that tells you what's changing?

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.405
And how should the vehicle
construct new plans

00:01:28.405 --> 00:01:30.155
in order to react to
that new information?

00:01:33.455 --> 00:01:34.830
Small air vehicles
are just going

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.306
to become part of our
everyday existence.

00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:39.930
They're going to be a new source
of information about the world

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.263
and they're going to be a new
way to move things around.

00:01:42.263 --> 00:01:45.520
And we're just going to take it
for granted 100 years from now.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.080
MINDELL: Aeronautical
engineering

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.260
is a funny kind of
engineering because it's

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.134
a really kind of systems
discipline from the beginning.

00:01:53.134 --> 00:01:54.550
ROY: There is the
system's problem

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.190
of how do you integrate
these vehicles safely?

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.780
How do you integrate
these vehicles reliably?

00:01:57.780 --> 00:01:58.995
How do they do all
the things they

00:01:58.995 --> 00:02:00.760
need to do without getting
in each other's way?

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:02.410
I mean, that's a
huge research problem

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:04.118
that's going to take
many years to solve.

00:02:04.118 --> 00:02:05.963
And I think there's
an opportunity for us

00:02:05.963 --> 00:02:08.360
as a department to really
be part of the solution.

00:02:13.362 --> 00:02:15.820
MINDELL: Airplanes are the glue
that knit the global system

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.260
together, but the focus is on
making them more efficient,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.460
on making the system more
efficient, making it more

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:22.501
environmentally friendly.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.596
URANGA: The idea was
design an airplane that

00:02:28.596 --> 00:02:32.590
could save 70% fuel.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:35.160
If you think about a fuselage,
it's one single bubble.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.630
And this one is two bubbles.

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.890
So the fuselage is wider.

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:39.910
It lifts more.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.280
Even the Wright brothers
had a wind tunnel

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.570
because we want to
know what's happening.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.980
Is this airplane
actually flying?

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:48.980
Is it going to lift off?

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:49.985
Is it not?

00:02:49.985 --> 00:02:51.860
And then you want to be
able to change things

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:53.480
in a controlled environment.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:54.985
All right, turn it on.

00:02:54.985 --> 00:02:57.110
So I'm going to go first
gear and then second gear.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.030
The tunnel is blowing 100
miles per hour on the model.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:09.150
We're actually having a
big computational effort.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.399
And they're actually simulating
exactly the conditions

00:03:11.399 --> 00:03:14.090
we're running in the tunnel.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:16.429
So you can validate things
for the computations

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:18.970
and then the computations can
do much more than you could do,

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:21.094
because your tunnel's
limited in speed or what not.

00:03:21.094 --> 00:03:22.810
But their computations isn't.

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:27.099
So, I think experiments,
wind tunnels, computations,

00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:28.640
you can make an
airplane out of that.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:29.730
STUDENT 1: Start at zero.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:30.230
Idle.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:31.590
Hold for two minutes.

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:32.950
Get the okay.

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.000
Increase power to 14,500.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:38.000
MINDELL: MIT's model for
a research university

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.880
has always been that the best
way to educate students is get

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.800
them involved in the research.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.350
URANGA: Okay,
tunnel speed is off.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.510
Shutting down the tunnel.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:51.350
I tell you, I have
a bet with a friend

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.710
that this thing is going to
fly in the next 20 years.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.080
OKELLO: Each person
in this department

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.790
has some kind of drive within
them, some kind of problem

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.490
that they want to solve.

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.370
LOHMEYER: The SPHERES project is
one of the really cool projects

00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:07.830
in space systems lab.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.630
They actually have
satellites inside the ISS

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.130
that they can control and
send different algorithms

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:18.529
to to improve the way that we
design satellites and operate

00:04:18.529 --> 00:04:19.712
with the astronauts.

00:04:19.712 --> 00:04:22.280
ASTRONAUT: Like Craig and
Katie, I studied science, math,

00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:24.550
and engineering at
MIT and it's taken me

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.885
on a wonderful path of
discovery and adventure.

00:04:32.318 --> 00:04:33.942
STUDENT 2: We have
two different motors

00:04:33.942 --> 00:04:35.400
and the way the
hitch is controlled

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:39.400
is by a servo that can tilt
the entire kind of axis.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.360
PERAIRE: The main strength of
the department are its people.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:47.460
Its faculty, its students,
its industrial partners,

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:48.280
its alumni.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.480
It's really the
community we have been

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.690
fortunate to be able to create.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:54.440
STUDENT 3: You're going
to go a lot faster

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:57.900
if you go straight, as opposed
to if you're zig-zagging.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:03.444
So control is really what makes
or breaks this competition.

00:05:03.444 --> 00:05:05.110
ROY: The best part
of each and every day

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.280
that I have here is
meeting with my students,

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.130
working through
research problems.

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.835
We are incredibly
fortunate at MIT

00:05:11.835 --> 00:05:14.017
to have the best
students in the world.

00:05:14.017 --> 00:05:16.100
OKELLO: It's the people
around you that definitely

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.850
keep you going and that's
one of the main things

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:20.910
I love about this department.

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:23.890
PERAIRE: It's really very
humbling to see what people

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.210
before us have done.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:28.100
NARRATOR: MIT is best known for
the scientists and engineers

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.320
who have studied there.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.740
PERAIRE: It started
with a vision,

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.190
but then we have
been tremendously

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:35.390
fortunate to attract the people
we've been able to attract.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:39.050
Whether it is Hunsaker,
Dolittle, Draper.

00:05:39.050 --> 00:05:43.210
Tremendous individuals
who've made major advances.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:48.150
ROY: This kind of basic research
into control and estimation,

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:51.670
sensing, et cetera, has been
a part of our department's

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.710
history for many, many years.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:58.780
The instrumentation lab at
Draper, the Apollo missions,

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.540
were all things that came
out of this department,

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:05.382
and it's great to be
continuing that tradition.

00:06:05.382 --> 00:06:07.090
PERAIRE: We've been
fortunate to graduate

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:09.130
a large number of
astronauts, whether it

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:11.904
is Apollo or the space shuttle.

00:06:11.904 --> 00:06:13.070
It's something that happens.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.110
We do not have a
deliberate attempt

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:17.740
to really attract
astronauts, per se.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:21.200
I think it's a byproduct of the
way we do research and the way

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.340
we do our work.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.930
MINDELL: Still to this
day, if young people say,

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.040
what do I have to do
to become an astronaut?

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.650
Go get a PhD at MIT is a pretty
common answer they'll hear.

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:35.030
STUDENT 4: This length is
actually five millimeters.

00:06:35.030 --> 00:06:37.959
URANGA: People in this
department at MIT in AeroAstro,

00:06:37.959 --> 00:06:40.250
in particular, they're very
excited about what they do.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.192
NEWMAN: Okay.

00:06:41.192 --> 00:06:42.650
URANGA: Who gets
to go to the moon?

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:44.570
Who gets to launch
a rocket, right?

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.262
Who gets to design
the next airplane?

00:06:46.262 --> 00:06:48.220
And I think that's what
you have to do in life,

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:49.660
you have to do what you like.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:52.460
People do it because
it was their dream.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.290
NEWMAN: So we're trying to
enable astronaut performance

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.670
on the moon or Mars,
get back to the planets.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:58.900
And it's kind of extreme.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.520
We're really going to be
going up very steep terrain,

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.770
we're going to be going
down, looking at craters.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:07.940
How can we design space suits
and life support systems

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:10.602
to enable that exploration?

00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:12.060
The current
traditional space suits

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:15.020
are big gas
pressurized balloons.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.930
They're very immobile,
they're very heavy.

00:07:17.930 --> 00:07:20.230
We kind of flip that and say
no, no we want a 20 kilo.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:23.660
In order of magnitude
reduction in mass, for sure.

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.560
As well as maximizing
the mobility.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.420
If I'm going to design a suit
that applies pressure directly

00:07:30.420 --> 00:07:33.670
over your skin, essentially I'd
like it to be a second skin.

00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:35.530
That's the real
research question,

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:37.210
how can I make a second skin?

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.640
We need to apply a
third of an atmosphere,

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.861
so our design goal
is 30 kilopascals.

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:44.360
That's the same pressurization
of the gas pressurized suits,

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:46.660
so we're trying to match
the current system.

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.410
But now get rid of the gas,
get rid of the balloon,

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.250
put the pressure
directly on the skin.

00:07:51.250 --> 00:07:54.760
And we think this patterning
is really important.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:58.000
I like to describe it lots
of times as a smart zipper.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:58.610
So, yeah.

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.890
You don on this, you put
it on, it's comfortable,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:02.370
but maybe a little bit loose.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.450
If you apply it in the
active materials and it

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:07.875
shrinks it up to get to
our desired pressure state.

00:08:10.209 --> 00:08:12.750
They generate actually a lot of
force when they're activated.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.300
This is just
stainless steel wire,

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:19.930
but now imagine
this was the shape

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.330
memory alloys that we
just played around with.

00:08:23.330 --> 00:08:26.680
So, if I open this
up, it's going

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:29.570
to give you a couple extra
centimeters here, to don.

00:08:29.570 --> 00:08:31.740
You know, to jump into, right?

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.252
Rather than me doing
it mechanically,

00:08:34.252 --> 00:08:37.704
the shape memory alloy, when
is activated under the voltage

00:08:37.704 --> 00:08:38.954
or the heat, it cinches it up.

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.880
MINDELL: Space is not quite
the technological frontier

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.427
it was in the '50s where it
was a bit of a wild west.

00:08:48.427 --> 00:08:50.260
The challenges that
we're left with in space

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.380
are really, very hard
challenges and they may not

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.840
be solved by new inventions.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.220
They may be solved by
kind of some end runs

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.890
around some of the
difficult problems.

00:08:58.890 --> 00:09:01.490
MILLER: I think in 60 years,
we'll have been on Mars.

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.260
Becoming a multi-planet
species, getting out

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.680
of the cradle of
Earth, permanently.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:09.020
OKELLO: Soon we may
overpopulate the Earth.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:11.110
We may actually need
to look outward.

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.860
MILLER: We're going
to be able to point up

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:14.276
at skies in our
local neighborhood

00:09:14.276 --> 00:09:17.630
and say, there are water
worlds there, there, and there.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:19.090
Is there intelligent
life on them?

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:20.600
How do we communicate with them?

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.016
And how are we
going to get there?

00:09:24.111 --> 00:09:26.610
BUTCHIBABU: We're going to have
a lot of intelligent systems

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:27.380
in the future.

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:32.762
And more automation and
more intelligent automation.

00:09:32.762 --> 00:09:34.970
OKELLO: Archeology is like
you know, discovering more

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:39.390
about our past and understanding
of where we've been.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.460
And I kind of see AeroAstros
like understanding

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:43.480
of where we can go.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

